config VL53L9CX
	bool "STMicroelectronics VL53L9CX time-of-flight sensor"
	default y
	depends on DT_HAS_ST_VL53L9CX_ENABLED
	select I2C
	select NRFX_GRTC if SOC_SERIES_NRF54LX
	select PWM
	help
	  Driver for the VL53L9CX dToF LiDAR module (up to 54x42 zones) over
	  I2C, wrapping ST's VL53L9 driver from X-CUBE-53L9A1. ST's sources are
	  vendored under st/ — see st/PROVENANCE.md.

	  PWM is selected unconditionally because the driver can source AP_CLK
	  from the MCU (the pwms property). On a board with its own oscillator
	  that costs a little flash and nothing else, a better trade than a
	  conditional select that fails obscurely on the first build.

if VL53L9CX

config VL53L9CX_INIT_PRIORITY
	int "Init priority"
	default 90
	help
	  Must be after the I2C, GPIO and (where used) PWM drivers. Not tied to
	  CONFIG_SENSOR_INIT_PRIORITY because this driver deliberately does not
	  use Zephyr's sensor subsystem — see include/vl53l9cx/vl53l9cx.h.

config VL53L9CX_POWER_SETTLE_MS
	int "Milliseconds to wait after enabling the sensor rail"
	default 500
	help
	  Time between driving power-gpios high and doing anything else.

	  Deliberately far longer than any plausible rail rise (Victor,
	  2026-09-05). The point during bring-up is to remove the delay from the
	  list of suspects entirely, not to be efficient: if the sensor still
	  does not answer after half a second of settled rail, the cause is
	  somewhere else and this can stop being a question.

	  The first bring-up used 10 ms, which is fine for a fast load switch
	  and not obviously fine for anything else.

	  This is paid on EVERY PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON, so it lands directly
	  in the wake cost that stage 4 measures. Trim it with a scope on the
	  rail before the energy work, not by guessing — and record the measured
	  rise time when you do.

config VL53L9CX_XSHUT_LOW_MS
	int "XSHUT reset pulse width, milliseconds"
	default 50
	help
	  How long XSHUT is held low. ST's reference timing, via the
	  hardware-validated community driver.

config VL53L9CX_XSHUT_SETTLE_MS
	int "Milliseconds to wait after releasing XSHUT"
	default 50
	help
	  ROM boot time before the part will answer. ST's reference timing.

	  The device NAKs while its ROM comes up, which is expected rather than
	  a fault — the driver's probe retries against a deadline for exactly
	  this reason, so this value being slightly short is recoverable.

config VL53L9CX_EXPOSURE_MS
	int "Exposure time per frame, milliseconds"
	default 8
	range 1 100
	help
	  Passed to vl53l9_set_exposure(), which programs NB_SHOT_STEP_n - the
	  number of VCSEL shots the device integrates per step.

	  THE DRIVER DID NOT CALL THIS UNTIL 2026-09-10, so the register sat at
	  its reset value of ZERO. Frames still arrive with zero shots, but zones
	  with a weak return have nothing to integrate and come back invalid,
	  which is exactly what the first working captures showed: 73 of 120
	  zones valid, the rest reported as no-target.

	  8 ms is ST's own value for their long-range profile
	  (VL53L9_USECASE_AR_RANGE, vl53l9_utils.c:37); their profiles span 4 to
	  10 ms. More exposure means more valid zones at distance and better
	  ambient rejection.

	  It is also the most direct energy knob on the whole device: exposure is
	  time with the VCSEL firing, so this multiplies the active-mode energy
	  the paper is about. Sweep it against valid-zone count before settling
	  on a number, and record both.

config VL53L9CX_LOG_BOOT_TIME
	bool "Log firmware blob upload duration at init"
	default y
	help
	  The 9,865-byte blob upload happens on every cold start and takes
	  roughly 250 ms at 400 kHz. Its duration decides whether powering the
	  sensor domain down during idle beats keeping it in standby, so it is
	  a measurement rather than a debug line. Leave this on — it is also
	  available at runtime via vl53l9cx_last_boot_ms().

config VL53L9CX_HOLD_HFCLK
	bool "Also hold the high-frequency clock on for AP_CLK"
	default n
	help
	  Fallback, not the primary fix. The thing that actually stops the GRTC
	  fast clock output is the SYSCOUNTER going to sleep, and the driver
	  requests SYSCOUNTER ACTIVE unconditionally to prevent that.

	  If the 8 MHz is STILL intermittent with that request in place, turn
	  this on: it additionally holds the high-frequency clock domain up, so
	  the divider always has something to divide.

	  Off by default because it is expected to be redundant, and a redundant
	  clock request inflates idle current — which is a number this project
	  has to measure honestly. See docs/plan/ap-clk-always-on.md.

module = VL53L9CX
module-str = VL53L9CX
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # VL53L9CX
